From 7e1817e6f2222adecb5db8f4973b3e2e3e1b8e94 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Thomas Klemm <github@tklemm.eu>
Date: Thu, 5 Nov 2026 10:25:59 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] Note in the upgrade guide that PostgreSQL 18.4+ fixture
 loading takes more locks

On PostgreSQL 18.4+, disable_referential_integrity switches every
foreign key to NOT ENFORCED and back, which takes about eight locks per
foreign key until commit. Parallel test workers that load fixtures at
the same time can fill the shared lock table with the default
max_locks_per_transaction.
---
 guides/source/upgrading_ruby_on_rails.md | 28 ++++++++++++++++++++++++
 1 file changed, 28 insertions(+)

diff --git a/guides/source/upgrading_ruby_on_rails.md b/guides/source/upgrading_ruby_on_rails.md
index 697bd23e7d..f0411fbfc7 100644
--- a/guides/source/upgrading_ruby_on_rails.md
+++ b/guides/source/upgrading_ruby_on_rails.md
@@ -176,6 +176,34 @@ Book.not_published # => [book2]
 Book.not_published # => [book2, book3]
 ```
 
+### Loading fixtures on PostgreSQL 18.4+ takes more locks
+
+On PostgreSQL 18.4 and later, Active Record turns off foreign key checks while it
+loads fixtures or empties the test database by switching every foreign key to
+`NOT ENFORCED` and back, instead of disabling triggers. This no longer requires a
+superuser, but each switch locks the constraint, its triggers, the referencing
+and referenced tables and their indexes until the transaction commits. In a
+typical schema that is about eight locks per foreign key in the database, where
+disabling triggers took about one per table.
+
+PostgreSQL keeps all locks in one shared table, sized from
+`max_locks_per_transaction` (64 by default) times the number of server
+processes, mostly `max_connections`. When parallel test workers load fixtures
+at the same time into databases with a few hundred foreign keys, they can fill
+it, intermittently and more often with more workers, and fail with:
+
+```
+PG::OutOfMemory: ERROR:  out of shared memory
+HINT:  You might need to increase "max_locks_per_transaction".
+```
+
+Raise the setting on the PostgreSQL server that runs the tests, for example in
+`postgresql.conf`, and restart the server:
+
+```
+max_locks_per_transaction = 256
+```
+
 Upgrading from Rails 8.0 to Rails 8.1
